To blend different audio files and create a realistic experience of a summer day using Java programming language, you can use libraries such as SoX, PortAudio, or JLayer. These libraries allow you to read, manipulate, and write audio files in various formats.

Here's a simple example using JLayer:

```java
import javax.sound.sampled.AudioFileFormat;
import javax.sound.sampled.AudioFileFormat.Type;
import javax.sound.sampled.AudioInputStream;
import javax.sound.sampled.AudioSystem;
import java.io.File;
import java.io.IOException;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

public class AudioBlender {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<File> audioFiles = new ArrayList<>();
        audioFiles.add(new File("birds.wav"));
        audioFiles.add(new File("leaves.wav"));
        audioFiles.add(new File("cicadas.wav"));
        audioFiles.add(new File("children_playing.wav"));

        List<AudioInputStream> audioStreams = new ArrayList<>();
        for (File file : audioFiles) {
            try {
                audioStreams.add(AudioSystem.getAudioInputStream(file));
            } catch (IOException e) {
                e.printStackTrace();
            }
        }

        AudioInputStream mixStream = AudioSystem.createMixedStream(audioStreams);
        try {
            AudioSystem.write(mixStream, AudioFileFormat.Type.WAVE, new File("summer_day.wav"));
        } catch (IOException e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
        }
    }
}
```

