The error you're encountering is due to an `InputMismatchException` being thrown while trying to read input from the user. This exception occurs when the input provided by the user cannot be parsed as the expected data type (in this case, an integer).

To fix this issue, you can add a check to ensure that the input provided by the user is valid before attempting to parse it as an integer. Here's an example of how you can modify your code to handle this:
```java
import java.util.Scanner;

public class RetrieveInputExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner scanner = new Scanner(System.in);

        System.out.print("Enter an integer: ");
        String input = scanner.nextLine();

        try {
            int number = Integer.parseInt(input);
            System.out.println("You entered: " + number);
        } catch (NumberFormatException e) {
            System.out.println("Invalid input. Please enter a valid integer.");
        }
    }
}
```
In this modified version of the code, we first read the input from the user using the `Scanner.nextLine()` method. We then wrap the code that attempts to parse the input as an integer in a `try` block. If the input is not a valid integer, the `NumberFormatException` will be caught, and an error message will be displayed.

This approach ensures that your program does not crash due to an `InputMismatchException`, and provides a more user-friendly experience for the user.

