To fix the CORS error in Next.js when using WordPress as the backend, you can follow these steps:

1. Install the necessary packages:

```bash
npm install --save cors axios
```

2. Create a new file named `cors.js` in your `pages/api` directory.

3. Add the following code to the `cors.js` file:

```javascript
import { NextApiRequest, NextApiResponse } from 'next';
import axios from 'axios';

export default async (req: NextApiRequest, res: NextApiResponse) => {
  if (req.method !== 'OPTIONS') {
    // Handle the actual API request here
    try {
      const response = await axios.get('https://your-wordpress-site.com/wp-json/wp/v2/posts');
      res.status(200).json(response.data);
    } catch (error) {
      res.status(500).json({ error: 'Something went wrong' });
    }
  } else {
    // Handle the OPTIONS request here
    res.status(200).json({
      Access-Control-Allow-Methods: 'GET',
      Access-Control-Allow-Headers: 'Content-Type',
    });
  }
};
```

Replace `https://your-wordpress-site.com` with the URL of your WordPress site.

4. In your `next.config.js` file, add the following configuration:

```javascript
module.exports = {
  // ...
  page: {
    async: () => {
      await require('./pages/api/cors');
    },
  },
};
```

This will ensure that the `cors.js` file is loaded before any other page in your application.

Now, when you make an API request from your Next.js application to your WordPress site, it should work without the CORS error.

